{
  "$comment": "VAPP-289 (docs/round-9-contract.md §9.3): the A2UI 1.0 regex dialect, HAND-WRITTEN. regex(value, pattern) = ONE hand-written parser + safety checker + backtracking matcher per core (TS src/regex.ts regexCheck, Rust eui-core src/regex.rs); no platform engine. Replayed by src/regex.test.ts (T) and the Rust core (R). CASE FORMAT: {name, pattern, value?, repeat?, expected, steps?}. `value` absent = the value is absent (undefined / missing arg). `repeat` = {pattern?: n, value?: n}: the string is repeated n times before the call (keeps the cap cases readable). `expected` = {valid: true|false} (the result) or {error: \"<message>\"} (an EXPRESSION_ERROR message, byte-exact). `steps` (optional) = the exact step count of the match (below); a core that counts steps compares it. ORDER: (1) a non-string pattern → `regex: pattern is not a string at 0`; (2) more than limits.maxPatternCodePoints code points → `regex: pattern longer than 256 code points`; (3) parse left to right, the FIRST error wins → `regex: <reason> at <offset>`, offset = the code point index in the pattern; (4) safety → `regex: rule <n>`, n = the LOWEST rule the pattern breaks (R2 before R3 before R4, anywhere in the tree); (5) the value: null/absent → \"\", a number → its JS text (String(n): 12, 1.5, -3), a boolean → \"true\"/\"false\", a string as is, an object or array → {valid: false}; more than limits.maxValueCodePoints code points → {valid: false} (no error); (6) search match → {valid}, or past limits.maxSteps steps → `regex: too expensive`. DIALECT (ECMAScript Pattern, u mode, no other flags, case-sensitive, over CODE POINTS: an astral character is one code point in pattern and value). Atoms: a literal code point (anything but ^ $ \\ . * + ? ( ) [ ] { } |); `.` = any code point but U+000A U+000D U+2028 U+2029; `[…]`/`[^…]` (`[]` matches nothing, `[^]` any code point; inside a class every code point but \\ ] is literal; `a-b` is a range when `-` is followed by anything but `]` (so `-` is literal first, last, as `\\-`, or right after a range: `[a-c-e]` = a–c, -, e); \\b in a class = U+0008); \\d = [0-9], \\w = [A-Za-z0-9_], \\s = U+0009–000D, 0020, 00A0, 1680, 2000–200A, 2028, 2029, 202F, 205F, 3000, FEFF; \\D \\W \\S = complements over U+0000–10FFFF; \\t \\n \\v \\f \\r; \\0 not followed by a digit; \\xHH; \\uHHHH (a lead surrogate escape directly followed by a trail surrogate escape \\uHHHH is ONE code point); \\u{H…} (any number ≥ 1 of hex digits, value ≤ 10FFFF); identity escapes of ^ $ \\ . * + ? ( ) [ ] { } | / (and - in a class). Assertions: ^ $ (input start/end only), \\b \\B (ASCII word = [A-Za-z0-9_]), (?=…) (?!…). Groups (…) (?:…), alternation |. Quantifiers * + ? {n} {n,} {n,m} (decimal; bounds saturate at 4294967295, n ≤ m compared exactly), each with an optional lazy ?. REASONS (every `regex: <reason> at <offset>`; offset in parentheses): `pattern is not a string` (0); `unmatched (` (the innermost unclosed `(`: `((a` → 1, `((a)` → 0); `unmatched )` (the stray `)`); `unmatched [` (the `[` of a class that reaches the end); `unmatched ]` (a `]` outside a class); `lone {` (a `{` that does not start {n} {n,} {n,m}); `lone }` (a `}` outside a class and not ending a quantifier); `nothing to repeat` (a * + ? or valid {…} quantifier where an atom is expected: pattern start, after | or (, after another quantifier and its lazy ?); `quantifier on assertion` (the quantifier after ^ $ \\b \\B (?=…) (?!…)); `quantifier range out of order` (the `{` of {n,m} with n > m); `range out of order` (the first endpoint of a class range with start > end); `class escape in range` (the first endpoint of a range with \\d \\D \\w \\W \\s \\S as an endpoint; the second endpoint is parsed first, so its own error wins); `backreference` (the \\ of \\1–\\9 or \\k outside a class); `lookbehind` (the `(` of (?<= or (?<!); `named group` (the `(` of any other (?<); `inline flags` (the `(` of any other (? that is not (?: (?= (?!); `property escape` (the \\ of \\p or \\P, in or out of a class); `control escape` (the \\ of \\c); `octal escape` (the \\ of \\0 followed by a digit, or of \\1–\\9 inside a class); `invalid escape` (the \\ of any other escape: a trailing \\, short \\x/\\u, \\u{} or an unclosed \\u{, \\- outside a class, \\B or \\k inside a class, any letter/digit/symbol not listed); `code point out of range` (the \\ of a well-formed \\u{…} above 10FFFF). Other messages: `regex: pattern longer than 256 code points`, `regex: rule 2`, `regex: rule 3`, `regex: rule 4`, `regex: too expensive` (catalog/limits.json `issues`). AST (the step count depends on it): a disjunction with one alternative is that alternative's CONCAT, with more it is ALT of the alternatives' CONCATs (every alternative is a CONCAT, also an empty or one-item one); the pattern, a group's and a lookahead's content are disjunctions; a literal, `.`, a class and \\d…\\S are one SET leaf; ^ $ \\b \\B are leaves; a GROUP (capturing or not) wraps its disjunction; a LOOKAHEAD wraps its disjunction; a QUANT wraps its atom. SAFETY: §9.3 R2–R4 over that AST (chars, nullable, first, unwrap as specified; unwrap removes groups and one-child concatenations/alternations). MATCHING + STEPS: ECMAScript backtracking order in continuation-passing form. One STEP = one call match(node, pos, k); the counter increments on entry, BEFORE the node does anything, and is shared by the whole call (all start offsets, lookahead sub-matches included). The calls: the search calls match(root, s) for s = 0, 1, …, len (inclusive) until one succeeds; SET: 1 step, consumes one code point if in the set; ^ $ \\b \\B: 1 step; GROUP: 1 step + match(child); LOOKAHEAD: 1 step + match(child) with an always-succeeding continuation, run once and atomically (its inner choices are dropped once it decides); CONCAT: 1 step, then match(item 0), and match(item k+1) each time item k succeeds (an empty CONCAT continues at once); ALT: 1 step, then match(branch 0), and match(branch i+1) only when branch i with the rest of the match failed; QUANT {min, max, lazy}: 1 step, then ECMAScript RepeatMatcher: max = 0 → continue; min > 0 → match(atom); greedy → match(atom) first, the continuation on failure; lazy → the continuation first, match(atom) on failure; after an iteration that ended at its start position while min = 0, that path fails (RepeatMatcher's empty check); each iteration lowers min (to ≥ 0) and max. Only match(atom) calls are steps: the continuation, the empty check and the RepeatMatcher recursion are not. More than limits.maxSteps steps → `regex: too expensive`. Examples: `ab` on \"ab\" = 3 (CONCAT, a, b); `b` on \"abc\" = 4; `^a+$` on \"aaaa\" = 9; `^a+?$` on \"aaaa\" = 11; `a|b|c` on \"zzc\" = 21 (ALT, CONCAT, leaf per branch). The step-cap cases are far past the cap (about 2^30 and n^4) and the under-cap case far below it (358,954), so they decide the same in any faithful count; the `steps` fields lock the exact count.",
